# REPRO: a layer restriction that starves a routing direction.
#
# `def_ndr <rule> layers <csv>` restricts the layers a governed bundle may be
# assigned.  A restriction naming only VERTICAL layers is perfectly legal and
# often what is wanted — a bus that routes purely vertically needs nothing
# else.  It becomes a fault only when the bundle's candidates need a
# HORIZONTAL segment and no horizontal layer is allowed: there is then no
# legal assignment, in any planner mode.
#
# ── What used to happen ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing.  The escalation ladder's last rung is BEST_EFFORT, which turns
# off the slide-window and overflow filters — but NOT the layer mask, which
# is checked unconditionally.  So every candidate stayed unscorable, the
# ladder fell through to a bare `continue`, and the bundle left the planner
# with no assignment and NO MESSAGE.  Downstream:
#
#   run_planner          silent for this bundle (every other rung warns)
#   run_nuts             "found no selected topology to place" — generic,
#                        names no bundle and no cause
#   run_detailed_nuts    hard error (or, in a mixed design, just fewer bits)
#   check_design         "Success: no violations found."
#
# A bus with no wire anywhere, and a clean audit.  That is the `flow/rv`
# item-10 failure shape reached through the planner instead of through
# generation, and it is what this vehicle pins.
#
# ── What happens now ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   [Planner] WARNING: Bundle N: NOTHING committed — this bundle gets no
#   layer assignment and no route. Its candidates need a HORIZONTAL segment,
#   but its allowed layers {M4,M6} contain no HORIZONTAL layer — ...
#
#   [Check] ... UNROUTED_BUNDLE: bundle N (...) has K candidate(s) but the
#   planner selected none — the bus has no layer assignment and no wire
#
# The planner names the cause; `check_design` is the safety net that refuses
# to call the design clean whatever the cause was.  Both are needed: the
# diagnosis is only reachable in the planner, and the audit must not depend
# on anyone having read the log.
#
# ── Why the restriction is NOT rejected at declaration ────────────────────
#
# `set_cell_layer_cap` hard-errors on a band with no H or no V layer, and
# copying that here was the obvious move.  It would be WRONG: a cell's band
# governs all of that cell's interconnect, which needs both directions,
# while an NDR rule governs named nets that may legitimately route in one
# direction only.  `ok_` below is exactly that case — same V-only
# restriction, vertically aligned endpoints, routes cleanly — and a
# declaration-time rule could not tell the two apart without knowing the
# placement.  So the check belongs where the direction is known, which is
# the planner.

add_block bad_a 0 0    200 200
add_block bad_b 800 600 1000 800

# Vertically ALIGNED, so this pair routes as a straight V run and the same
# V-only restriction is satisfiable.  The control that keeps the fix honest:
# a blanket declaration-time refusal would reject this legal design too.
add_block ok_a  0 1200 200 1400
add_block ok_b  0 1800 200 2000

add_bus bad_[3] bad_a.p bad_b.q
add_bus ok_[3]  ok_a.p  ok_b.q
add_bus free_[3] bad_a.d bad_b.e

def_layer 3 M3 H 20
def_layer 4 M4 V 20
def_layer 5 M5 H TOP 20
def_layer 6 M6 V TOP 20

def_track_pattern 3 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 4 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 5 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 6 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1

# One rule, two bundles, opposite outcomes — the restriction is identical
# and only the geometry differs.
def_ndr vonly width x2 layers M4,M6
set_ndr bad_ vonly
set_ndr ok_  vonly

run_bundler STRICT
generate_topologies
set_track_pitch 3
run_planner 1

run_nuts
check_design
report_wirelength

# This vehicle's PASS condition is that the fault is REPORTED — the flow
# test asserts on the planner warning and the UNROUTED_BUNDLE violation, and
# on bundles 1 and 2 routing normally.  The run still exits 0: `check_design`
# is report-only by design, and gating a run on its verdict is what
# `buda --strict-check` is for.
